# Managed by Ansible (roles/fail2ban).
#
# Requests that matched no configured vhost and fell through to the default
# site: bare-IP scanning, probes for the reverse-DNS hostname, and attempts to
# use the proxy as an open relay (requests for httpbin.org and friends).
#
# Two things about this format are easy to get wrong:
#
#  1. fail2ban strips the timestamp before matching, so the line the regex sees
#     starts with an EMPTY bracket pair: '[] 200 - GET http <host> "/uri" ...'.
#     A filter anchored on '^\[[^]]+\]' matches nothing, ever.
#  2. datepattern must cover the date ONLY. Include the surrounding brackets
#     and fail2ban removes '[...]' whole, leaving nothing for '^\[\]' to
#     anchor on — measured on real logs: 0 matches bracketed, 2459 unbracketed.
#
# fallback_access.log carries four fields before the vhost; the per-proxy-host
# logs carry six (extra cache/upstream status). One regex cannot serve both,
# which is why npm-login is a separate filter reading separate files.
#
# Layout: [] <status> <upstream> <method> <scheme> <host> "<uri>" [Client <ip>]
[Definition]
failregex = ^\[\] \S+ \S+ \S+ \S+ (?!(?:[A-Za-z0-9-]+\.)*{{ fail2ban_site_domain | regex_escape }} )\S+ "[^"]*" \[Client <HOST>\]
ignoreregex =
datepattern = %%d/%%b/%%Y:%%H:%%M:%%S %%z
